ueen side and one of  the positional threats is to put the  pawn 95 and grab even more space  so if he plays a move like c6 then a5 i  think is a decently strong move  so pawn a5 prevents me from playing a5  and we continue h3 rook e8 bishop b3  and bishop f8 and uh yeah so far  everything is very normal  and here i played an idea that  i usually like to play in online blitz  it's a little bit of a  tricky move is queen to b1 uh now i  should note the standard move is queen  d2  and he was actually telling me after the  game that he was  uh i guess a little bit more  fearful of this move given that uh it's  perhaps uh the best move objectively and  uh it's one of the reasons why this line  isn't so popular um i think he was  telling me like he was going to play c6  rook d1 knight b6  um  but still very much be a game  so i chose to play queen b1 because it  leads to  also a simple ideas to play queen a2  and create the battery against f7 pawn  now he takes i take back and he plays  knight e5  so the position transforms a little bit  in the center  and here i had a decision to make  whether to  take with the knight or the bishop or  maybe even a move like bishop e2 and i  decided to take with a bishop and even  though this concedes the bishop pair i  thought  i just have a very stable solid position  also with some initiative  not only am i attacking f7 but rook t1  is also coming with tempo  so  i wasn't feeling too bad around here  he defends queen e7 brook d1  c6  and  yeah so far i think it was like very  standard chess  and uh all my pieces are  developed and it was a question what to  do next and i decided to go for a very  typical plan is knight h4  buying the f5 square for the knight but  also eyeing the g6 square this is a  a potentially tricky idea that uh  because f pawn is pinned  to the king knight g6 is possibly a  positional threat to uh  eventually get black starks for a bishop  so he plays queen c7 removing the queen  from being a potential target  i play rook e3  still trying to optimize the pieces  maybe some ideas of rook lifting but  also ideas of just doubling up on d-file  he plays bishop b6  and i play rook d3 king h7  and in this position  i'm  sorry to say that uh i just  completely overlooked  a crucial idea in the position and my  next move turned out to be uh really  really really bad  even though when i played this move i  thought  it was a decently  good move  um but the move is not at five  and at first this looks natural  uh just improving the knight and eyeing  the d6 square  and when i played this move i was mainly  considering what would happen after  knight takes e4  as calculating an interesting line  bishop takes e6  i should note that if i take the knight  back then black ends up winning a pawn  so i was gonna take here first and  um i guess black has two options here  if pawn takes i take here takes and then  here  with ideas of knight f6 coming also  hitting the queen so this would be very  good for white  and the main line that i was considering  was  that take c3  after which i can take back with pawn  and even though i'm down upon  in this position  i think white just has a lot of  initiative  so i was going down the rabbit hole of  these lines  and  i didn't quite realize a danger in a  different line  yeah he ended up  not taking on e4 but taking on f5 and  this turns out to be really problematic  for me because after pandex f5  pawnee 4 is played  and i considered this uh  when i played knight f5 and i thought  okay i can just play rook e3 and target  the now possibly overextended pawn and  e4 and i thought okay i'll just double  up  and the pawn should be a weakness on top  of that  f7 is uh is just hanging here  so i saw this position from a distance  and thought it wasn't so bad  the thing that i missed is black just  has e3 coming  and  if i play rook e3 there's bishop c5  and it turns out to be  just really really  bad for white like white's whole  position can crumble very quickly  now i didn't play rook e3 and maybe i  should have uh even though after like  rookie one  pawnee three  it doesn't look so great for white  um instead i played rook t2 still  allowing pawn e3 and after he takes  he plays bishop c5  and  even though i'm temporarily up a pawn  and i can win  the pawn on f7  uh  when bishop takes e3 comes my king is  going to be running out of squares  especially because the dark spurs are  weak the queen is piercing through the  position  so i decided first to play king h1 here  getting off the diagonal of the bishop  he takes with a rook on e3  and then i took on f7  and then in this position  joe bava unleashes  a really crazy move that i did not see  coming and even after he played this  move  it took me  maybe a few minutes to fully understand  what was going on  he played knight g4  uh before he played this i thought  things were still like under control  because i was preventing knight h5 i was  preventing knight e4  but knight g4  is uh  just an insane  idea  which  leads to a really cool mating line  um  i i really don't have too many options  after this move because black is  fretting made in one  so  uh yeah it looks like i have to take the  knight and then  black sacrifices  more material here  and uh maybe if if you're watching and  you want to try and find the move  feel free to pause the video but this is  black to move sack more material and i'm  pretty sure it's just force mate for  black  so uh  yeah good luck finding the next move  that joe bava uncorked  the brilliant rook to e1  and yeah i i felt a pit in my stomach at  this point  uh realizing that he had just calculated  like everything else  and uh on top of that oh a small crowd  was forming around our table  uh because he he was in the midst of  sacrificing two pieces first a knight  than a rook  and even though i'm up a ton of material  here after queen to g3  there's just no defending against the  force mate  queen h4s and mate threats  and my pieces are just not in shape to  defend against uh  against the combination of the queen and  the bishop  so i thought for for several minutes  here just searching for any  any last opportunity  i do have a couple spike checks  but they don't really do anything  so i played the move rook d3  unfortunately this doesn't really help  because after queen h4 white is still  getting mated  and i resigned in this position  just to show how things could play out  if rook h3  there's queen takes e1 king h2 bishop g1  king h1 a small windmill and then queen  g1 would be made  so really nice game a really nice finish  by joe bava  to uh completely punish my uh my kind of  careless blunder  with knight f5 and i i think um  unfortunately this game it was really  determined by  by one blunder um  i have not checked things with the  engine but  we chatted a little bit after the game  and uh  yeah i think in this position  ah maybe white is slightly slightly  worse but uh okay it definitely could  have gone on probably should have taken  the bishop  and um i'd consider this actually during  the game rook takes e6  uh the knight should really probably  just return to f3  um or maybe here i can play that f5  so earlier did i have any any advantage  no engine already prefers back in this  position  if we go back even further  ah the engine wants knight takes e5  yeah i guess maybe i should have valued  the bishop pair  a little bit more and then play bishop  e3  probably close to equal here  and maybe one takeaway from this game is  i should make an effort to  actually understand the main line  uh queen d2  which i think is a more standard move in  this position  uh just preparing the simple rook d1  and perhaps if the queen uh  is is just more in the center than  uh then i wouldn't get brutally mated as  what happened in the game  so anyway this was a a quick and kind of  painful game but i hope it inspires  people watching  that  yeah joe bava is just a monster when it  comes to attacking